 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of Gaussians $N$ and pixels $P$, similar to standard Gaussian Splatting rendering $O(N \cdot P)$ or $O(N + P)$ depending on implementation details like spatial hashing [G].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the additional normal attribute per Gaussian [G].
-*   **Performance:** Rendering quality improves significantly with the number of Gaussians [S1, unofficial]. However, VoGE renderer gives slightly fuzzier boundaries compared to mesh renderers [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables lighting integration and normal-based supervision for Gaussian Splatting, which natively lacks surface information [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Normals are an approximation derived from the volumetric representation; boundaries are fuzzier than explicit mesh rendering [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of Gaussians $N$ and pixels $P$, similar to standard Gaussian Splatting rendering.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Additional memory required to store the normal vector $n_i$ for each Gaussian.
+*   **Performance:** Rendering quality improves significantly with an increased number of Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Allows for lighting integration in Gaussian Splatting pipelines [S1, unofficial]. Simple to implement by treating normals as an RGB attribute.
+*   **Limitations:** Boundaries may be fuzzier than mesh-based renderers [S1, unofficial]. Requires pre-computed normals if starting from a mesh.
 
 ## Background
-This method was introduced in the context of VoGE (Volume Gaussian Ellipsoids), a differentiable volume renderer using Gaussian ellipsoids for analysis-by-synthesis [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to bridge the gap between volumetric Gaussian representations and surface-based analysis tasks like lighting and normal supervision [S1, unofficial]. The approach leverages PyTorch3D for normal computation and lighting [S1, unofficial].
+This method was introduced in the context of VoGE (Volume Gaussian Ellipsoids), a differentiable volume renderer using Gaussian ellipsoids for analysis-by-synthesis [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the lack of native surface normal information in Gaussian ellipsoids, which are represented as volumes. The approach leverages PyTorch3D for normal computation and lighting integration [S1, unofficial].
 
